en:
That's why, when I see a sad painting, the tears just spill out on their own.
They say paintings suck out people's souls, don't they?
Haven't you ever heard a story like that?{p}

Well?
Lovely, isn't it?
Somehow, the painting looks happy.{p}

So the painting was taken down.
And it became one of the many works sleeping in the club room.
But then...{p}

Once it was taken back and hung in the art room, it changed back into the portrait it had been.
Strange, isn't it?
After that, nothing else happened.{p}

The painting slipped in, of its own accord, among the works piled up in the club room.
As if to hide from people's eyes.
After that, no one ever tried to pull it out again.{p}

I see.
I'm so glad to hear you say that.
I was sure that you, of all people, would say so.{p}

...Well?
How does it look to your eyes?
To mine...{p}

You're easily swayed by other people's opinions.
You should have a bit more of a mind of your own. You're not a child anymore.
Well, never mind.{p}

I love paintings too, you know.
I think it's because I have such a vivid imagination, but when I look at a painting, I feel as if I'm being drawn right into it.
And then I feel the painting's atmosphere with my whole body.{p}

With that, Iwashita turned her face away in a huff.
It didn't look like she would tell me anything more.
There was nothing for it. I'd go and hear the next person's story.{p}

That painting is a self-portrait, painted by a student who used to go to this school.
Of course, she was in the art club.
Her name was Tomoko Shimizu.{p}

They say she was a very quiet person.
She loved painting, and she was aiming to get into art college.
But she was murdered while she was still painting that self-portrait, apparently.{p}

...Something made a sound just now, didn't it?
What was that sound?
......Look!{p}

It was decided that it might be best to go ahead and burn it.
So they took it to the incinerator, but the painting was too big to fit inside.
There was nothing else for it, so they laid it on the ground and decided to set fire to it right there.{p}

...After that, no one ever suggested burning that painting again.
Of course not. Nobody wants to die.
And that's why that portrait is still in the art room today.{p}

...And that's the end of my story.
Now then, shall we go to the art room and put that painting back?
Who knows, we might hear something.{p}

...Here we are.
Ah, the painting is still hanging right there on the wall.
{a}{f:|}, sorry, but would you take that painting down off the wall for me?{p}

Iwashita said that with a big smile on her face.
It felt like she was making fun of {f:me|me}, and it got under my skin.
So, though I was a little scared, I walked up to the painting without letting it show.{p}

...The eyes moved!
The eyes of the woman in the painting looked at {f:me|me}☆
Everything else was oil paint, but those eyeballs alone were solid and real, just like a living person's.{p}

Iwashita was still smiling.
"Well then, let's go back.
I wonder who will tell us the next story?"{p}

This time, the teacher struck the match carefully.
Once it had properly caught, the teacher shielded it with one hand so the wind couldn't blow it out.
Then the teacher ever so gently brought it close to the painting.{p}

The next day, the painting vanished from her house as well.
One of the art club members heard the news, had a hunch, and went to the art room.
And there... it was.{p}

And it turned up at her house.
That's when everyone finally realized.
That painting was coming to school in place of Shimizu, who was dead.{p}

...But you know what I think?
I wonder if she wants to go and look for her family.
But shut away in a place like that, she can't do that, can she?{p}

...But when they called, no one answered.
No matter how many times they called back, they couldn't get through.
So, with no other choice, the art club's advisor and the club president went to the house, they say.{p}

And there......the uncle lay dead, blood pouring from his throat.
After that it was utter chaos, with the police being called and everything.
They say the uncle's wound had been made by the bite of some large animal.{p}

Maybe I should work out a way of my own, just in case, so I can take revenge whenever I need to... heh heh.
Well, that's the end of my story.
Who's next?{p}

......Yes, that's it.
I'm so glad you'll come after all.
It makes me happy.{p}

But {f:I|I} held firm.
She was putting on that knowing act on purpose{f:.|.}
She thought it would make {f:me|me} scared.
{f:I wasn't going to fall for a trick like that.|I wasn't going to fall for a trick like that.}
As she was closing the door, Iwashita gave me a sweet smile.
"You're more of a fool than I thought."{p}

Thinking I must have imagined it, I looked toward the desk, and there was the portrait.
It hadn't been there a second ago★
The blood all through my body froze solid.{p}

How do you come up with such things?
What goes on inside that head of yours?
I'm opening it.{p}
